{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791654018,"nonce":"s6EFqGQX6aygOEhvnTLxMUPJO1g=","title":"A culling scroll drops what is in view inside a nested scroll once it is scrolled","message":"In cn_demo the Bio textarea shows no caret, and its typed text is not drawn either, once the page is scrolled down to the form. The page is `scroll().viewport_cull(true)`; text_area keeps its lines and caret in a scroll container of its own.\n\nCause: the paint walker set the cull rect from the culling scroll's absolute layout bounds and tested each child at its absolute bounds plus `new_cumulative_scroll`. That cumulative scroll restarts at every scroll container (it serves sticky and fixed positioning), so a nested scroll's children were tested without the outer scroll's offset. Scrolled further than the 200 px overscan, everything inside the nested scroll fell outside the rect and was skipped: no paint, no canvas record. The painted-on-screen record that gates redraws had the same mix-up. text_input carets survived because a text input holds no scroll container. The event router's hit test was already right: it compares visual bounds on both sides (`test_hit_test_nested_scroll_after_outer_scroll_offset`).\n\nFound by driving cn_demo with synthetic CGEvent clicks and screen captures: the textarea's rebuild built the caret (focused=true), seven full paints followed, and the caret's parent was never painted while its grandparent, the nested scroll, was.\n\nFix: `RenderTree::paint_scroll`, the sum of every ancestor's scroll on the paint walk, never restarted (zeroed for a fixed child). The cull rect is placed on screen by its ancestors' scroll, and children and the on-screen record are tested with it. `tests/viewport_cull_nested_scroll.rs` covers paint, culling far below, and the on-screen record.","files":null}]}